    "body": "© Anekoho\nBy Alison Koo\n20 November 2026\nWorsening bottlenecks in South-east Asian ports have resulted in intra-Asia rates moving up, even as congestion in China eases.\nYesterday, Drewry’s Intra-Asia Container Index (IACI) edged up $4 from 12 November, to $1,444 per 40ft, Shanghai-Manila rates rose 7%, to $1,310 per 40ft, while Shanghai-Laem Chabang increased 2%. to $1,584 per 40ft.\nBut average vessel waiting times on the two routes increased four hours and two hours, respectively.\nCongestion intensified at major transhipment hubs, particularly Singapore, pushing rates from Shanghai to the port up 1%, to $2,064 per 40ft.\nThe Loadstar’s checks with Chinese ecommerce platform Pinduoduo showed customers had been advised to expect delays of around a week, due to vessel bunching around Singapore – Pinduoduo’s app noted: “Due to several ships docking in Singapore simultaneously, there have also been delays in unloading cargo.”\nThe easing of logjams in Chinese ports was reflected in the decline in intra-North Asia rates. Shanghai-Busan prices fell 8%, to $990 per 40ft, while Shanghai-Kaohsiung rates fell 3%, to $1,257 per 40ft, as average vessel waiting times decreased by three hours at Busan and four hours at Kaohsiung.\nAhead of the peak season for intra-Asia shipping, carriers have been deploying more capacity. Ferry operator Panstar Line, which piloted a Busan-Europe service through the Northern Sea Route in August, is starting a Busan-Incheon-Shekou-Hai Phong routing with compatriot feeder operator Pan-Continental Line next month.\nThe PHX service will see Panstar deploying the 700 teu Dongjin Fortune , which will be chartered from compatriot feeder operator Dong Jin Shipping, and complements Pan-Continental’s 1,000 teu Pancon Harmony . Panstar plans to replace the Dongjin Fortune with a 1,000 teu chartered vessel next year.\nPanstar started out in liner shipping in 2024, with a South Korea-Japan feeder service. Showing ambitions of expanding its liner presence, it joined the Korea Near-Sea Freight Conference in July, a league of feeder operators active on the South Korea-Japan route.",
